From: Noxwell <beebono@yahoo.com>
Date: Mon, 29 Jun 2026 04:50:03 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 121/225] arm64: dts: sprd: ums512: add aliases to pin mmc/i2c
 indices

Upstream's ums512.dtsi has no aliases block, so MMC devices were numbered
by probe order and the eMMC drifted to mmcblk1. The initramfs pivots to
the Debian rootfs on mmcblk3p74, so it must stay mmcblk3. Pin mmc0=sdio0
(microSD) and mmc3=sdio3 (eMMC), and the i2c bus indices, matching the
6.16 tree.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/sprd/ums512.dtsi | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sprd/ums512.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sprd/ums512.dtsi
index 45621ee5a..b56912508 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sprd/ums512.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/sprd/ums512.dtsi
@@ -13,6 +13,22 @@ / {
 	#address-cells = <2>;
 	#size-cells = <2>;
 
+	aliases {
+		/*
+		 * Pin the MMC indices: eMMC must stay mmcblk3 (rootfs lives on
+		 * mmcblk3p74) and the microSD mmcblk0, independent of probe
+		 * order. Without these the kernel auto-numbers by probe order
+		 * and eMMC drifts (e.g. to mmcblk1).
+		 */
+		mmc0 = &sdio0;
+		mmc3 = &sdio3;
+		i2c0 = &i2c0;
+		i2c1 = &i2c1;
+		i2c2 = &i2c2;
+		i2c3 = &i2c3;
+		i2c4 = &i2c4;
+	};
+
 	cpus {
 		#address-cells = <2>;
 		#size-cells = <0>;
